From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 19:46:11 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 299/342] meson_dwmac: the stall's explanation is an inference,
 and OSF cured it

With operate on second frame: 16 fresh boots, 24 GB, no stall, where builds without it
stalled within their first two boots, and sending at 64 MB/s against 51.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../drivers/network/ether/meson_dwmac/meson_dwmac.cpp  | 10 +++++-----
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/add-ons/kernel/drivers/network/ether/meson_dwmac/meson_dwmac.cpp b/src/add-ons/kernel/drivers/network/ether/meson_dwmac/meson_dwmac.cpp
index 08626b7406..58967a562e 100644
--- a/src/add-ons/kernel/drivers/network/ether/meson_dwmac/meson_dwmac.cpp
+++ b/src/add-ons/kernel/drivers/network/ether/meson_dwmac/meson_dwmac.cpp
@@ -896,11 +896,11 @@ MesonDwmac::_ConfigureEee()
 }
 
 
-/*!	The transmit engine can stay suspended with frames waiting, whatever the
-	poll demands say: it read ahead while the last frame was still going out,
-	and that frame's status then closed the next descriptor instead of its own.
-	One slot is left owned by the engine behind its position, the next one has
-	lost its OWN bit, and the engine rightly won't go on from it.
+/*!	Without OPERATION_OSF the transmit engine stayed suspended with frames
+	waiting, whatever the poll demands said: the slot behind its position was
+	still its own, the one at its position had lost OWN with its length intact,
+	as if a frame's status had closed the next descriptor. Not seen since OSF is
+	set; this stays for anything like it.
 
 	After 2 s of that, gives the ring back to the engine in order, from the
 	oldest frame. A frame that had gone out may go out again, which TCP copes
